This is the show that asks

00:00:16.610 --> 00:00:18.320
scientists the big questions

00:00:18.440 --> 00:00:20.089
in the time it takes to travel up

00:00:20.090 --> 00:00:21.789
and down 45 floors.

00:00:26.510 --> 00:00:27.510
Hey, Tara.

00:00:27.810 --> 00:00:29.719
I hear that you're going to expand

00:00:29.720 --> 00:00:31.429
my mind with some information about

00:00:31.430 --> 00:00:32.557
the expanding universe.

00:00:32.670 --> 00:00:34.009
Yes. Do you think my mind can take

00:00:34.010 --> 00:00:35.749
it? I hope so. Can it expand as much

00:00:35.750 --> 00:00:36.529
as the universe?

00:00:36.530 --> 00:00:37.530
No.

00:00:38.510 --> 00:00:39.619
OK, let's learn.

00:00:44.320 --> 00:00:45.594
I'm a bit scared of lifts.

00:00:46.065 --> 00:00:47.065
Let's set the lift right first.
You're

00:00:47.770 --> 00:00:48.549
scared of lifts?

00:00:48.550 --> 00:00:49.749
A little bit. Well, this is a great

00:00:49.750 --> 00:00:50.799
place for you to be.

00:00:51.940 --> 00:00:53.655
OK, well, let's go! So the universe

00:00:53.860 --> 00:00:55.209
is expanding. What is that about?

00:00:55.450 --> 00:00:56.979
When we looked at galaxies in the

00:00:56.980 --> 00:00:58.659
early 20th century, we realised that

00:00:58.660 --> 00:00:59.829
they were moving away from us.

00:00:59.830 --> 00:01:01.479
And the further they were, they were

00:01:01.480 --> 00:01:02.259
moving faster.

00:01:02.260 --> 00:01:03.530
So the universe is expanding.

00:01:03.550 --> 00:01:05.099
What is that expansion?

00:01:05.110 --> 00:01:05.919
What's happening there?

00:01:05.920 --> 00:01:07.251
Is more matter being created?

00:01:07.510 --> 00:01:08.882
No, the space between matter

00:01:09.550 --> 00:01:11.379
is expanding, but only on the

00:01:11.380 --> 00:01:12.249
larger scale.

00:01:12.250 --> 00:01:13.839
So if you imagine some dough with

00:01:13.840 --> 00:01:15.310
raisins in put in the oven and

00:01:15.340 --> 00:01:16.761
heated up, the dough expands.

00:01:17.770 --> 00:01:19.209
And so all the raisins are moving

00:01:19.210 --> 00:01:20.529
away from each other because the

00:01:20.530 --> 00:01:21.930
space between them is expanding.

00:01:22.060 --> 00:01:23.649
So the raisins are planets and the

00:01:23.650 --> 00:01:25.230
dough is just empty space.

00:01:25.270 --> 00:01:26.270
They're not planets they're groups

00:01:26.560 --> 00:01:27.560
of galaxies.

00:01:27.580 --> 00:01:29.319
Our solar system is joined together

00:01:29.320 --> 00:01:30.320
because of gravity.

00:01:30.760 --> 00:01:32.919
And is that gravity lessening?

00:01:33.250 --> 00:01:34.671
No. So gravity actually holds

00:01:35.140 --> 00:01:36.879
our solar system together and our

00:01:36.880 --> 00:01:38.497
galaxy and our group of galaxies.

00:01:39.130 --> 00:01:40.845
It's the space between them that is

00:01:40.930 --> 00:01:41.930
expanding.

00:01:43.240 --> 00:01:43.989
And we're halfway through.

00:01:43.990 --> 00:01:44.859
Oh wow.

00:01:44.860 --> 00:01:45.999
That was quick. Yeah, that was

00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:47.485
really quick. OK, where were we?

00:01:47.570 --> 00:01:49.349
So groups of galaxies are moving.

00:01:49.540 --> 00:01:50.949
Why are they moving? What's causing

00:01:50.950 --> 00:01:51.399
this?

00:01:51.400 --> 00:01:52.989
We think it's a property of space

00:01:52.990 --> 00:01:54.313
itself. The vacuum of space

00:01:55.030 --> 00:01:56.739
has a kind of weird energy that we

00:01:56.740 --> 00:01:57.740
call dark energy.

00:01:57.790 --> 00:01:59.590
We think it makes up 70 percent of

00:01:59.620 --> 00:02:01.384
the universe and it's what's causing

00:02:01.510 --> 00:02:03.225
the universe to expand and actually

00:02:03.550 --> 00:02:05.139
accelerate. So is that dark energy

00:02:05.140 --> 00:02:06.939
filling those spaces or is it just

00:02:06.940 --> 00:02:08.319
causing that push? It seems to be

00:02:08.320 --> 00:02:09.643
part of the nature of space

00:02:10.479 --> 00:02:11.439
itself.

00:02:11.440 --> 00:02:13.059
It means that ultimately we're going

00:02:13.060 --> 00:02:14.889
to have a really sad, lonely

00:02:14.890 --> 00:02:16.458
death. As the groups of galaxies

00:02:16.990 --> 00:02:18.819
get further apart, the universe

00:02:18.820 --> 00:02:20.409
will kind of become cold and lonely

00:02:20.410 --> 00:02:21.410
for each of them.

00:02:22.030 --> 00:02:23.619
And so that's kind of our inevitable

00:02:23.620 --> 00:02:25.659
end. We'll die a really sad death.

00:02:27.060 --> 00:02:28.236
That's really terrifying

00:02:29.040 --> 00:02:30.510
and not at all where I thought

00:02:31.080 --> 00:02:32.109
this was going to go.

00:02:32.520 --> 00:02:34.529
Right, but the good thing for humans

00:02:34.650 --> 00:02:36.419
is that the Sun is halfway through

00:02:36.420 --> 00:02:38.086
its life and in about another four

00:02:38.340 --> 00:02:40.229
billion years, it will have expanded

00:02:40.230 --> 00:02:41.945
to be a giant star and it will have

00:02:42.060 --> 00:02:43.530
boiled the oceans and burnt up

00:02:43.680 --> 00:02:44.939
everything on Earth. So we won't be

00:02:44.940 --> 00:02:46.319
around for the sad, lonely death.

00:02:46.470 --> 00:02:47.759
Well, you've given me much to think

