expanding.

00:03:04.140 --> 00:03:05.855
But the big thing is that it's also

00:03:06.270 --> 00:03:07.319
accelerating.

00:03:07.530 --> 00:03:09.539
And I will admit, I stole a little

00:03:09.540 --> 00:03:11.370
bit of Tara's time outside of

00:03:11.400 --> 00:03:12.959
the elevator because I really wanted

00:03:12.960 --> 00:03:14.789
to know more about how we know

00:03:14.790 --> 00:03:15.840
this, how we measure it.

00:03:16.050 --> 00:03:17.849
And I found out about a little thing

00:03:17.850 --> 00:03:18.879
called redshift.

00:03:19.080 --> 00:03:20.844
So basically what this is, it's kind

00:03:21.060 --> 00:03:22.709
of like measuring the Doppler effect

00:03:22.710 --> 00:03:23.639
with sound, right?

00:03:23.640 --> 00:03:24.899
So, you know, you hear an ambulance

00:03:24.900 --> 00:03:25.979
coming towards you and it gets

00:03:25.980 --> 00:03:27.213
higher pitch than lower and it goes

00:03:27.214 --> 00:03:28.214
yewww.

00:03:28.650 --> 00:03:30.860
The same thing happens with light.

00:03:31.050 --> 00:03:32.765
So as things move further away from

00:03:32.970 --> 00:03:34.587
us and we're looking at them, the

00:03:34.590 --> 00:03:36.569
frequency gets lower and it shifts

00:03:36.570 --> 00:03:37.589
towards red.

00:03:37.740 --> 00:03:39.839
And because of this, we can also

00:03:39.840 --> 00:03:41.759
see that it's moving faster

00:03:41.760 --> 00:03:44.219
and faster. And that's really

00:03:44.220 --> 00:03:46.189
exciting to take a look at.

00:03:46.200 --> 00:03:48.089
Now, will the universe

00:03:48.090 --> 00:03:49.659
